{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfyCJYVBJMSroVhwLDQHB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RDM5ZGjN2FgzzTr76zbkK5","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q015_artifact_tag_not_homology","answer":"In SCOPe 2.08, d1a14h2 (sunid 343393, description '1a14 H:1-8') is an N-terminal expression/purification tag segment, not a native protein domain. It is classified in class l (Artifacts) > fold l.1 'Tags' > superfamily l.1.1 'Tags' > family l.1.1.1 'Tags' > protein 'N-terminal Tags' > species 'Synthetic'. The main chain segment d1a14h1 (residues 9-111, sunid 20061) is a real domain in a different class, b.1.1.1 (all-beta, immunoglobulin-type). The 1a14 chain H is a single polypeptide that SCOPe split into a real domain and a tag.\n\nSimilarity to the tag should NOT be treated as protein homology evidence. The 'Tags' fold, superfamily and family in class l are a bookkeeping category for engineered, non-native sequence that has been grouped for convenience. They do not describe a shared evolutionary ancestor, fold or function. Eight residues of a tag are too short to give a significant structural or sequence match. A match to a tag most likely means a shared vector, construct or purification-tag design, such as a His-tag or linker. It does not mean the two proteins are related. The grouping gives no basis for inferring function, fold, template suitability or phylogeny. For homology of 1a14, use the real domain d1a14h1 in b.1.1.1 and apply the usual family and superfamily criteria. Do not use the artifact class l, and exclude it when choosing representatives, templates or training or benchmark sets.","evidence_used":["/api/v1/releases","/api/v1/releases/2.08","/api/v1/search?q=d1a14h2&release=2.08","/api/v1/search?q=d1a14h1&release=2.08","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/343393","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/311501","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/310894","/api/v1/releases/2.08